\n            tokens.append(('LPAREN', '('))\n            i += 1\n        elif expr[i] == ')':\n            tokens.append(('RPAREN', ')'))\n            i += 1\n        elif expr[i].isspace():\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            # Operators like +, -, *, /\n            tokens.append(('OP', expr[i]))\n            i += 1\n    return tokens\n```\n\nI'm facing challenges with parsing negative numbers. The current tokenization approach needs refinement to handle unary minus correctly. I'll need a more nuanced method to distinguish between subtraction and unary negation.\n\nMy strategy will involve carefully tracking the parser's context when encountering negative signs. This means checking if we're at the start of an expression or after an operator, not just in the middle of a term.\n\nThe recursive descent parser will handle this by creating specific parsing functions for different precedence levels - one for addition/subtraction and another for multiplication/division.",
